      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Itta_Bena%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1846, families from Claiborne County sailed up the Yazoo River seeking fertile farmland that would become Itta Bena.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.worldatlas.com/cities/itta-bena-mississippi.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Georgia Pacific Railroad bought land through the plantation in 1888, helping the village expand.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.worldatlas.com/cities/itta-bena-mississippi.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the 1966 civil‑rights march between Itta Bena and Greenwood, Stokely Carmichael coined the phrase “Black power.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
